  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge of high pressure remains in control with a warm \nsector regime prevailing through the week. High temperatures in the \n90s and dewpoints that are once again overachieving in the 70s will \nyield heat index values up to near 110 degrees through at least \nThursday. Model guidance has trended weaker with a cold front that \neventually washes out over the FA on Friday. Little relief is now \nexpected that yields heat index values remaining above 100 degrees \nwell into the weekend. As a result, the heat headlines may \neventually need to be extended with a 597 dm ridge building over the \nFA from the south. The height rises fall well in the 99th percentile \non the NAEFS for this time of year with 850 mb temps warming to 25C. \n\nDespite the trend for a stronger ridge of high pressure, multiple H5 \nshortwaves are still progged to round the ridge across the Ohio \nValley that will bring rain chances in the vicinity beginning today. \nThe NSSL/NAMNest try to bring in a MCS as early as this morning, but \nmost of the CAMs including the HRRR do not show much convection \nclipping the northeast until Tuesday evening at the earliest. In \nfact, Wednesday evening and potentially Thursday evening continue to \nbe when the risk is the greatest for a potential MCS complex with \nNBM PoPs maxing out around a 40-50%. However, the probability is non-\nzero for something else to sneak in outside this window at some \npoint. \n\nThere remains the potential for stronger storms with a consistent \nsignal to support some severe weather, especially on Wednesday.\nMLCAPE each day is progged around 2500 to 3500 J/kg by the \nRAP13. Combined with steep 8-9 C/km sfc-3km lapse rates and \nDCAPE upwards of 1400 J/kg, damaging winds will be the main \nconcern with any complex of storms that develops. Although the \nbest upper level dynamics are located over the Great Lakes \nregion with a 120 kt jet max, the thermodynamics are more than \nsufficient to make up the modest deep layer shear in the \nprofile. It is worth noting while northeast portions of the FA \nmay see heavy rainfall, the south may see no rainfall at all. \nLow rain chances may linger into Friday, followed by dry \nconditions over the weekend as the aformentioned ridge \nsuppresses additional chances.",
